# The Dockerfile ends in `COPY . .`, so anything left in apps/backend at build
# time is baked into the image. `.env` is the one that matters: it is gitignored,
# which means it never shows up in review, and .env.example actively invites
# somebody to create one right here. The moment that happens it ships inside the
# image — readable by anyone who can `docker run` it or pull it from a registry.
#
# Nothing was leaking when this was written (apps/backend/.env does not exist).
# This exists so that stays true.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Copied and then immediately replaced by the deps stage — pure build cost, and
# a host-built tree can carry the wrong platform's native binaries.
node_modules

# Never needed at runtime; .git would carry the whole history into the image.
.git
.gitignore
test
coverage
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Demo seeding. deploy/README.md says "production builds strip it" and until now
# that was not true: the Dockerfile ends in `COPY . .`, so the re-seed script and
# the demo SQL it reads were baked into the production image. It cannot RUN there
# — index.js gates it on ALLOW_DEMO_DATA_REFRESH and refuses outright when
# NODE_ENV is production — but code that writes demo businesses, branches, staff
# and tickets has no business being inside a production image at all, and the
# require() in index.js is wrapped in a try/catch precisely so it can be absent.
# The file stays in the repo because the e2e suite runs it from a checkout.
scripts/refresh-demo-data.js
scripts/sync-demo-test-accounts.js
